.\" Manual page for segler-desktop.
.\"
.\" It exists because the binary takes an argument and has no --help to
.\" describe it: src/main.rs reads args_os().nth(1) and nothing else, so this
.\" is the only place that argument is written down, and lintian tags a
.\" binary without a page.
.\"
.\" @VERSION@ is substituted by packaging/debian/build-deb.sh, the same way
.\" control.in is, so the version in the header cannot drift from the package.
.\" The date is the date this page was last revised and not the build date,
.\" which is the convention and is also what makes the page reproducible.
.\"
.\" Author: David M. Anderson
.\" Built with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic)
.TH SEGLER\-DESKTOP 1 "2026\-09\-04" "segler\-desktop @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
segler\-desktop \- editor for DocLang documents
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B segler\-desktop
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Segler
opens a DocLang document or archive and shows it as a reader would see it:
headings, paragraphs, lists, tables and pictures in reading order, with the
element tree on the left and the selected element's properties on the right.
Text is edited in the rendered document; labels, layers, levels, bounding
boxes and table cell kinds are edited in the element pane. Every edit can be
undone. What is saved is the file that was opened with the edits applied and
nothing else changed: markup that was not touched is written back byte for
byte, and an archive's page images and assets are carried across untouched.
.PP
It is a windowed application, normally started by opening a document from a
file manager rather than from a shell. The package registers the two DocLang
media types, the desktop entry and the icons that make that work.
.SH OPTIONS
There are none.
.B segler\-desktop
is a window rather than a command\-line interface, and it takes one optional
positional argument and nothing else. For a command\-line interface to the
same library, see
.BR segler (1).
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I file
The document to open: a
.I .dclg
file holding DocLang markup, or a
.I .dclx
archive. With no argument the window opens empty and offers to choose one.
.PP
A file that cannot be read is reported in the window rather than on the
standard error, because the argument usually arrives from a file manager
where nobody is watching a terminal.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns success when the window has been closed. A non\-zero status means the
window could not be created at all \(em no display, or no usable graphics
driver \(em rather than anything about a document.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/mime/packages/doclang.xml
The two media types, which are what make a file manager offer this
application for a document. Matched by filename: neither the markup nor the
archive reserves magic bytes of its own.
.TP
.I /usr/share/applications/segler\-desktop.desktop
The desktop entry.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR segler (1),
.BR xdg\-mime (1),
.BR update\-desktop\-database (1)
.PP
The format is specified in spec.md in the DocLang repository at
https://github.com/doclang\-project/doclang, and the design of this
application in DESIGN.md in its own.
.SH AUTHOR
David M. Anderson
